  "sid": {
   "DAG": "Directed acyclic graph: nodes joined by arrows with no directed cycle. The object SID compares.",
   "CPDAG": "Completed partially directed acyclic graph: represents a whole Markov equivalence class at once, so some edges stay undirected because the data cannot orient them.",
   "PDAG": "Partially directed acyclic graph, allowing both directed and undirected edges. SHD is defined over these, which is why it covers DAGs as a special case.",
   "d-separation": "A purely graphical test for whether conditioning on a set blocks every path between two nodes. Blocks chains and forks at a conditioned node, and opens a collider when the collider or one of its descendants is conditioned on.",
   "collider": "A node with two arrowheads into it on a path. Blocked by default, and opened by conditioning on it or on any of its descendants -- the reverse of every other node's behaviour.",
   "Markov": "A distribution is Markov with respect to a graph when the graph's d-separations imply the distribution's conditional independences. SID scores correctness over the whole class of distributions Markov to the true DAG.",
   "intervention distribution": "The distribution of one variable when another is set by intervention rather than observed, written p(y | do(X = x)). What SID compares, in place of edges.",
   "adjustment set": "A set of variables to condition on so an intervention distribution can be computed from observational data. SID uses the estimated graph's parent set by default.",
   "parent set": "The direct causes of a node in a graph. Always a valid adjustment set in the graph it comes from, which is why SID uses it -- it is cheap and local.",
   "mediator": "A node on a directed path between the intervened node and the target. Adjusting for one, or for its descendants, destroys the effect being measured -- condition (a) of the adjustment criterion.",
   "pre-metric": "Satisfies non-negativity and identity but not symmetry. SID is one: swapping the true and estimated graph can change the value.",
   "idempotence": "Applying an operation twice changes nothing after the first time. Path-matrix squaring reaches this once the closure is complete, which is why a bounded number of squarings suffices.",
   "transitive closure": "The reachability relation: an entry is true when a directed path exists, not just an edge. Computed by repeated squaring of the adjacency matrix.",
   "SHD": "Structural Hamming Distance: counts the variable pairs whose edge type differs between two graphs. The edge-counting baseline SID is contrasted against.",
   "Markov equivalence class": "The set of DAGs implying exactly the same conditional independences, and so indistinguishable from observational data alone. Summarised by a CPDAG.",
   "SID": "Structural Intervention Distance: a metric for comparing an estimated causal graph to a true graph by counting, for each ordered pair of variables, whether the adjustment set implied by the estimated graph correctly predicts the interventional distribution under the true graph. It is proposed as an alternative to edge-counting metrics like SHD because it directly measures causal-inference correctness rather than structural similarity.",
   "adjustment-set": "A set of variables (typically a node's parents) used to block confounding paths between a source and target so that conditioning on it yields the correct interventional distribution via the back-door criterion. SID is built on adjustment sets derived from an estimated graph's parent sets and checked against the true graph's paths.",
   "SEM": "Structural Equation Model: a system of equations, one per variable, expressing each as a function of its parents plus independent noise. The paper uses linear Gaussian SEMs to attach concrete causal effects to a DAG so that intervention predictions can be computed and used to test adjustment sets in SID.",
   "causal-effect": "The change in a target variable's distribution induced by intervening on (setting) another variable, as opposed to merely observing it. SID's score is the count of pairs whose causal-effect prediction, computed from the estimated graph's adjustment set, disagrees with the true graph.",
   "non-causal": "Describes a path between two nodes that is not a directed causal route from source to target (e.g., a path running through a common cause). A valid adjustment set must block every such non-causal path while leaving the true causal path open.",
   "PC": "The PC-algorithm, a constraint-based causal discovery method that outputs a CPDAG (a Markov equivalence class of DAGs) rather than a single DAG, since equivalence classes are all that the observational distribution alone can identify.",
   "GES": "Greedy Equivalence Search, a score-based causal discovery algorithm that, like the PC-algorithm, returns a CPDAG (Markov equivalence class) instead of a single DAG.",
   "back-door": "The back-door path/criterion from causal inference: a non-causal path between source and target running through a common ancestor, which a valid adjustment set must block. In SID, a matching parent set between estimated and true graphs automatically satisfies this criterion.",
   "causal-discovery": "The task of learning a causal graph's structure from data (e.g., using the PC-algorithm or GES). The paper discusses how SID and related metrics are used to evaluate and score the output of causal-discovery methods.",
   "edit-distance": "A distance measure defined by the minimum number of elementary edit operations (e.g., edge insertions, deletions, reversals) needed to transform one object into another. The paper characterizes SHD as this kind of edge-based edit-distance metric, contrasted with SID's intervention-based comparison.",
   "non-descendant": "A node that cannot be reached from another node via any directed path. Intervening on a non-descendant of Y (such as a parent of Y) cannot alter Y's distribution, since observational and interventional distributions coincide for non-descendants.",
   "non-directed": "Describes a path between two nodes that is not a directed path from source to target. A valid adjustment set must block every such non-directed path between source and target without itself lying on the directed causal path.",
   "parent-set": "The set of a node's direct parents (immediate causes) in a graph. SID uses an estimated graph's parent-set for a given source node as the candidate adjustment set to test against the true graph."
  },
